<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salamanca</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Salmantica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salamanca is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, on the River <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tormes">Tormes</a>. As Salmantica it was a town of the Vettones that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> attacked in 220 BCE and later a Roman settlement on the road known as the Via de la Plata. Its university, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX of León</a> around 1218, is the oldest in Spain still operating and was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/school-of-salamanca">School of Salamanca</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salamanca.jpg" alt="Salamanca" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva from Cacau · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:68436-Salamanca_(49093713952)_edited.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Salamanca lies on the northern half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meseta-central">Meseta Central</a>, in the west-central part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, straddling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tormes">Tormes</a> river, in the comarca of Campo Charro. It is the capital of the Province of Salamanca and stands 64 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a>, 121 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> and 123 kilometres from Portugal. The three small hills on which settlements grew, formed by the erosion of two streams, and the nearness of the river suited the early functions of defence and control of the surrounding land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hilltop site of Cerro de San Vicente, on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tormes">Tormes</a>, is the first stable settlement, dating to the 7th century BCE. By the 3rd century BCE a settlement at the Teso de las Catedrales had consolidated under the influence of the Vaccaei and Vettones, Celtic tribes. After Roman conquest it became the civitas of Salmantica. After the Roman period the Alans, then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, held it, and in 589 it was an episcopal seat that sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> to the councils of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. Christian resettlement under the Kingdom of León, led by Raymond of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, took hold in the 11th century.</p><p>Salamanca joined the revolt of the Communities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1520 against the taxes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, and afterwards the king had the upper parts of the towers of the rebels' palaces removed. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a> the city was occupied by the troops of Marshal Soult in 1809 and stayed in French hands until the Battle of Arapiles in 1812, when an Anglo-Portuguese army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> defeated Marmont's French army. The French destroyed much of the city for building materials, including the university's colegios mayores.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-salamanca">University of Salamanca</a> received its royal charter as an Estudio General from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX of León</a> in 1218. It was the first institution to receive the title of university, in 1254, and under Alfonso X its schools of canon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> drew students even from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>. Antonio de Nebrija's grammar of Spanish appeared here in 1492, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> studied here briefly before leaving in 1501.</p><p>The Old City was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1988. It holds the Baroque Plaza Mayor, designed by Alberto and Nicolás Churriguera, the Casa de las Conchas, whose facade carries about 350 scallop shells, and two cathedrals, the Romanesque Old Cathedral of the 12th century and the much larger Gothic New Cathedral, begun in the 16th century and completed in the 18th. The city had 144,458 inhabitants in 2024 and its Holy Week processions are of international tourist interest.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Salamanca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The universities of <b>Salamanca</b> and Alcalá, where Cardinal Francisco Jiménez de Cisneros sponsored the Complutensian Polyglot Bible, nurtured the School of Salamanca, whose theologians such as Francisco de Vitoria debated the rights of conquered peoples and helped lay the foundations of international law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The city of León, seat of the court and a bishop, and later Astorga, Zamora, <b>Salamanca</b> and Santiago de Compostela were the main centres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It took in the lands of the Lusitanians, the Vettones around <b>Salamanca</b> and Ávila, and the Celtici of the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Elio Antonio de Nebrija dated the introduction to his Gramática de la lengua castellana, written in <b>Salamanca</b> and the first grammar of a modern European language, writing that language was always the companion of empire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-18">18 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Vettones emerged in the second half of the first millennium BCE between the Douro and Tagus rivers, in modern Ávila, <b>Salamanca</b> and Cáceres and parts of Portugal.</blockquote><a href="/state/vettones">Vettones</a></li></ul></section>
